<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carita Parahyangan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carita Parahyangan is an Old Sundanese palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, usually dated to the late 16th century. It narrates the history of the Sundanese kingdoms of Galuh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> from the 7th century to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, listing kings and the lengths of their reigns. Though it mixes legend with history, it is one of the main sources for pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Carita Parahyangan</b>, a Sundanese text of the late 16th century, makes Sanjaya Sanna's son, tells how Sanna was defeated by Purbasora of Galuh and how Sanjaya avenged him and ruled West, Central and East Java and Bali; romanticised as it is, its agreement with the Canggal inscription…</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Sundanese tradition, chiefly the 16th-century <b>Carita Parahyangan</b>, Galuh emerged around 669 when the lands of Tarumanagara divided along the Citarum River, Galuh taking the east and the Sunda Kingdom the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/galuh-kingdom">Galuh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
